    <p>Two of the fastest-rising Storyteller roles, content strategist and developer relations, have no standalone government category, so they hide inside the writing and public relations numbers. Worth noting too: training and development specialists, who make ideas land so people learn, are projected to grow 11%, much faster than average, evidence that making a message stick is a rising market (<a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/training-and-development-specialists.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BLS, 2025</a>). Best Answer Hub tags several of these amplified, because AI drafts the words and the human supplies the judgment.</p>

    <div class="cav">The evidence</div>
    <h2 id="value">Why does framing actually work?</h2>
    <p>Because how you frame a thing changes what people do about it, and a Storyteller controls the frame. The clearest demonstration is a study on charitable giving: told about a single identifiable child in need, people gave an average of $2.12, but told the same need as a statistic about millions affected, they gave just $1.21, roughly half, for the identical cause (<a href="https://www.cmu.edu/dietrich/sds/docs/loewenstein/SympathyCallous.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Small, Loewenstein and Slovic, 2007</a>). Nothing changed but the framing, and the response nearly doubled.</p>
